{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 48, :comments ["<b>After he is sold, redemption shall be his.</b> Now would it enter your mind that he could be redeemed before he is sold? What does the verse teach by saying 'after he is sold'? For since it was already stated, 'you shall not rule over him with rigor in your sight' (Leviticus 25:53), you might have thought that this is the essential reason for his redemption, and if so, I would wait until I observe his conduct toward him, whether he subjugates him or not. It thereby teaches us that immediately after he is sold, even though he has not yet worked under him at all, you are obligated to redeem him — so that he not learn from his deeds and say, 'Since my master commits sexual transgressions and worships foreign gods and desecrates the Sabbath, I too will be like him.' And if we say that every 'after' (אַחֲרֵי) implies a lapse of time, Scripture uses 'after' for an amplification: even if he was already with him for many years and you saw that he does not subjugate him with rigor, even so you are obligated to redeem him, so that he not learn from his deeds."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:38:35.414766253Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}